CAKE, with L4S marking, for routers whose Qualcomm PPE offloads the flows (qca_ppe). The hardware keeps routing, NATing and encapsulating offloaded connections, and hands their frames to a qdisc on the way out, so every flow that crosses the shaped link is shaped by CAKE: its own shaper, flow and host isolation, COBALT with ECN marking and NAT awareness, plus CE marks for L4S packets (ECT(1)) once they queue longer than 3 ms (ce_threshold, marked with a probability that rises to one at twice the threshold). Upload is shaped on the uplink device on the WAN port (pppoe-wan, a VLAN device or the port itself); download on an IFB that both the host's ingress redirect and the driver feed. Set this on the physical WAN port (wan). The per-packet overhead defaults to 50 bytes (preamble and gap, Ethernet, one VLAN tag, PPPoE); a configured link layer (linklayer, overhead, mpu) replaces it, and iqdisc_opts and eqdisc_opts are appended to the download and upload CAKE. Without CAKE's ce_threshold in the kernel and tc, CAKE shapes as usual without the L4S mark, and the log says so. Costs CPU for each shaped packet, spread over all cores.
